# Copy to .env and fill in. **This file holds credentials and nothing else.**
#
# Everything else — the Vault, the Skills, the bounds, the model, the connectors, the
# GitHub App — is configured in `open-agent.config.json`, which names these variables and
# never contains their values. See docs/configuration.md, and
# open-agent.config.example.json for a worked example. The full setup guide (the Slack
# manifest, the App manifest, the scopes, and the documented traps) arrives with build/13.
#
# The one exception, because it cannot live inside the file it points at:
# CONFIG_PATH=./open-agent.config.json

# --- Slack -------------------------------------------------------------------
# From your own Slack app (create it from the manifest; do not use a shared app).
# Bot User OAuth Token, under OAuth & Permissions. Checked at startup with auth.test.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
# App-Level Token with connections:write, under Basic Information. Socket Mode.
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-...

# --- GitHub, if you want it --------------------------------------------------
# A **GitHub App**, not a personal access token (ADR-0006). You register it, install it,
# and pick the repositories the coworker may reach in GitHub's own UI — that picker is the
# real boundary, and nothing in this instance can widen it. Install it once and never touch
# GitHub authentication again: the private key has no expiry, and the one-hour installation
# tokens are minted on demand.
#
# Remove the `github` section from the configuration file if you are not using GitHub;
# startup will say so and carry on.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=1234567
# The `.pem` GitHub downloaded when you generated the key. A **path**, not the key itself:
# a multi-line PEM inside a .env gets mangled by quoting, and every mangling looks like a
# signing bug. Keep the file outside this repository.
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=/Users/you/.secrets/open-agent.private-key.pem

# --- Connectors, if you want any --------------------------------------------
# Each connector in the configuration file names the variable holding its bearer token.
# Codex reads that variable itself — the wrapper is not in the tool path (ADR-0005) — so
# the token never enters this process.
# LINEAR_API_KEY=lin_api_...
